tmux is the bus. o is the table. no plumber.

the decision

one dispatcher, bin/o, reached by every producer. one resolver, sh/p,
for text whose meaning is not yet known. ~/lib/plumbing stops being a
second table and becomes a shim into p. nothing new runs.

the drift that raised this — djvu evince against djvu zathura, mp3 mpv
against mp3 mocp — is two owners of one table, and the fix for two
owners is one owner (root). a router removes no owner; it adds a third
participant.

the bus was never vacant

tmux is not a sibling of ed and fm, it is what they run inside: a
long-lived user server with a namespace, addressable objects, and a way
to hand a message to one of them. that is the plumber's job, already
filled and already paid for.

	plumber           tmux
	the daemon        the server, running regardless
	$NAMESPACE        /tmp/tmux-$uid/{default,...}, $TMUX, -L
	ports             panes: %57, {marked}, {last}
	plumb/send        set-option -p, run-shell, send-keys
	attr name=value   pane options: @p
	wdir              #{pane_current_path}
	click             #{mouse_x}, #{mouse_word}
	plumb start       run-shell -b
	plumb client      split-window, spawn -t

conf:16 sets @p and p:19 reads it — a name=value pair carried out of
band so screen text is never a shell command. that is a plumb message,
built for plumb's reason, arrived at without it. p:56 sends to {marked}
else {last} — a port you point at and can see, whose failure is text in
the wrong prompt and not a message into nowhere.

a second daemon duplicates the transport and is blind to the first
one's objects. that blindness is the cost in full: a plumber started
from the X session cannot name a pane, so plumb start e cannot split
the right one. named attributes are not rule variables — $attr is the
whole string — so the pane crosses back as text for another script to
unpick. a script to recover what tmux gives away.

the rules file cannot hold the rules

the language is is, isdir, isfile, matches, set, add, delete, and to,
client, start. no shell predicate. git rev-parse at p:65, the a/ b/
strip at :38 and the repo-root second guess at :35 cannot be written in
it and stay in p. inverting does not move the decision, it splits it:
the easy half becomes rules, the hard half does not move, and there are
two owners again, one level down.

the shape

	tmux/conf   gesture    which text, which pane   needs the grid
	sh/p        resolve    text → object            needs a shell
	sh/o        dispatch   object → program         needs a name

each layer needs what its owner has and nothing else does. plumb wants
resolve and dispatch in one language and can do half of resolve. one
mechanism per thing beats three composed through a router (simple).

the change

	type is text
	data matches '[a-zA-Z0-9_\-./:~@#]+'
	plumb to open
	plumb start p $0

untested; nothing is running to test it against. an edit rule ahead of
it if acme should open files itself. acme's button 3 then gets the same
chain as right-click. delete the file outright if acme never opens —
the change is a deletion either way (terse).

what is given up

extent by pattern. plumb takes the longest leftmost match containing
the click; word-separators " " is the cheap version and it is global,
so double-click and every copy-mode word motion move with it. if it
ever bites: mouse_x and copy_cursor_x into p, matched against p's own
patterns — same idea, no daemon. address:11 named it.

delivery to a running program with no pane. tmux can start one, never
hand a message to one already open. answered per program where it
matters — o:18 is mocp -S then mocp -l, a plumb client rule written by
hand for the one program that needed it, kept in the dispatcher.

revisit when

a gesture source outside tmux meets a consumer that must be reached
while already running. a second gesture source alone changes nothing;
it execs p, as tmux does. ed with a look chord would need p -w first —
p takes wdir from the pane (p:24), and a pane running ed reports ed's
cwd, not the edited file's.

-CC is a different question

control mode is a text protocol: commands in, %begin/%end out, %output
%pane <bytes> and notifications alongside; -CC is that with echo off,
what iTerm2 attaches with. it moves who draws and who holds the mouse,
not who decides. the client takes raw bytes and must be a terminal, and
having no tty it never fires tmux's mouse bindings — mouse_word,
copy_cursor_word, copy-mode, copy-pipe all go. it is the road to owning
the grid. address:1 turned that down.
